<h2>A thirty-year occupation, a sixteen-year suit</h2>
<p>The defendants had occupied the Naraina plot since 1994 for an iron and steel business; the agreements lapsed in 2002&ndash;2004, a quit notice followed in 2005, an arbitration produced an award in 2006 that was set aside in 2010, and the possession suit (CS(OS) 1453/2010) worked its way to a decree on admissions in 2026. The appeal consolidated every surviving defence: want of a Section 106 TPA notice, the Rent Control bar, res judicata, and non-joinder of a co-owner.</p>

<h2>The four holdings</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Co-owner may sue.</strong> The defendants themselves admitted the plaintiffs were co-owners under the 1982 perpetual lease deed; one co-owner can maintain a suit for possession, so non-joinder failed.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>No &ldquo;premises&rdquo;, no DRC bar.</strong> Open land with a temporary tin shed is outside Section 2(i) of the Delhi Rent Control Act; the Section 50 bar on civil jurisdiction never engaged.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Licence, not lease.</strong> Occupation against commission of Rs 25 per metric tonne, with no fresh agreement after 2004, showed an intention to create a licence; and once the DRC Act was out, the label mattered little &mdash; the occupants held under the plaintiffs either way.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Res judicata rejected.</strong> An award set aside on arbitrator-independence grounds adjudicates nothing on merits; its findings cannot bar a later suit.</div>
</div>

<h2>The part that will be cited: admissions and the dead award</h2>
<p>The appellants&rsquo; best point was conceptual: if the award died, surely the arbitration record died with it. The court separated the two. The <em>findings</em> of the arbitrator ceased to bind when the award was set aside; the <em>admissions</em> the defendants themselves made in their counter-statement of 1 April 2006 &mdash; acknowledging the commission arrangement &mdash; remained their own statements, usable against them like any other admission. Their written statement&rsquo;s contrary story &ldquo;stands belied by their own Counter-Statement&rdquo;. On such clear and unequivocal admissions of the material facts &mdash; ownership, the character of occupation, the expiry of any authority to remain &mdash; Order XII Rule 6 was properly invoked, because a trial on admitted facts is an exercise in futility.</p>

<h2>Lessons for property litigation</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Plead with the future in mind.</strong> Every pleading in every forum &mdash; arbitration included &mdash; is a potential admission in the next proceeding. Setting aside the decision does not unsay the pleading.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Characterise the occupation early.</strong> Commission-linked, business-facilitation occupation of open land points to licence; a tenancy defence needs rent receipts and a &ldquo;premises&rdquo; within the DRC Act.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Order XII Rule 6 is a real endgame.</strong> Delhi&rsquo;s civil courts increasingly decree possession on admissions where the defence is inconsistent with the defendant&rsquo;s own documents &mdash; and the High Court will sustain it in RFA.</div>
</div>
<div class="note">This article reports a judgment of the High Court of Delhi for general information only. It is not legal advice and does not comment on any pending proceeding.</div>
